{
  "sn16.3:0.1": "Linked Discourses 16.3 ",
  "sn16.3:0.2": "1. Kassapa ",
  "sn16.3:0.3": "Like the Moon ",
  "sn16.3:1.1": "At Sāvatthī. ",
  "sn16.3:1.2": "“Mendicants, you should approach families like the moon: ",
  "sn16.3:1.3": "withdrawn in body and mind, ever the newcomer, and never rude. ",
  "sn16.3:1.4": "Suppose a person were to look down at an old well, a rugged cliff, or an inaccessible riverland. ",
  "sn16.3:1.5": "They’d withdraw their body and mind. ",
  "sn16.3:1.6": "In the same way, you should approach families like the moon: ",
  "sn16.3:1.7": "withdrawn in body and mind, always the newcomer, and never rude. ",
  "sn16.3:2.1": "Kassapa approaches families like the moon: ",
  "sn16.3:2.2": "withdrawn in body and mind, ever the newcomer, and never rude. ",
  "sn16.3:2.3": "What do you think, mendicants? ",
  "sn16.3:2.4": "What kind of mendicant is worthy of approaching families?” ",
  "sn16.3:2.5": "“Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” ",
  "sn16.3:3.1": "Then the Buddha waved his hand in space. ",
  "sn16.3:3.2": "“Mendicants, this hand is not stuck or held or caught in space. ",
  "sn16.3:3.3": "In the same way, when approaching families, a mendicant’s mind is not stuck or held or caught, thinking: ",
  "sn16.3:3.4": "‘May those who want material things get them, and may those who want merit make merits!’ ",
  "sn16.3:3.5": 